<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Auto Backup on API 26-30; data_extraction_rules.xml is the same decision for 31+.

   The database and the settings prefs are excluded on purpose, not left in by the
   exclude-only default: a reinstall has to be genuinely empty, and "Delete
   everything" has to actually be the end of the data, not something a Google
   restore quietly undoes. beyondborders.db (and its -wal/-shm write-ahead files)
   and beyondborders.settings.xml — which also carries has_seen_welcome — are the
   two places that would otherwise bring the whole travel history and app state
   back on the next install. widget_prefs.xml goes too: it is keyed by AppWidget
   id, and the launcher re-allocates those from scratch after a reinstall, so a
   restored entry could dress a brand-new widget in a stale config.

   The travel history is not lost to Auto Backup, only kept from restoring itself
   automatically: a plain JSON snapshot written to files/cloud/snapshot.json (see
   CloudSnapshot) rides along instead, and it sits there unread until the user taps
   Restore in Settings — the same import path a picked file already goes through.

   beyondborders.device.xml is excluded for an unrelated reason: it holds the
   per-install id that a future sync uses to tell its own writes from another
   device's, and restoring it would give two installs the same id — each ignoring
   the other's edits as an echo of its own. Excluded, it is regenerated on first
   launch, which is the correct answer for a new install.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="database" path="beyondborders.db"/>
    <exclude domain="database" path="beyondborders.db-wal"/>
    <exclude domain="database" path="beyondborders.db-shm"/>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="beyondborders.settings.xml"/>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="widget_prefs.xml"/>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="beyondborders.device.xml"/>
</full-backup-content>
